d Death in Neurons. The Molecular Biology of the Neurone</article-title><year>2003</year><fpage>435</fpage><lpage>464</lpage>2nd edn., pp</citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shiels</surname><given-names>PG</given-names></name><name><surname>Jardine</surname><given-names>AG</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Dolly, no longer the exception: telomeres and implications for transplantation</article-title><source>Cloning Stem Cells</source><year>2003</year><volume>5</volume><fpage>157</fpage><lpage>160</lpage><!--PubMed citation query: 'Cloning Stem Cells||5|157||bib24|'--><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12930628</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Vaziri</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Dessain</surname><given-names>SK</given-names></name><name><surname>Ng Eaton</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Imai</surname><given-names>SI</given-names></name><name><surname>Frye</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name><name><surname>Pandita</surname><given-names>TK</given-names></name><name><surname>Guarente</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Weinberg</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>hSIR2(SIRT1) functions as an NAD-dependent p53 deacetylase</article-title><source>Cell</source><year>2001</year><volume>107</volume><fpage>149</fpage><lpage>159</lpage><!--PubMed citation query: 'Cell||107|149||bib25|'--><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11672523</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Sirtuin expression in serially passaged mammary epithelial cell cultures. The figure shows relative sirtuin gene expression in serially passaged cells relative to <italic>18&#x02009;s rRNA</italic> gene expression. Mean data are plotted &#x000b1;s.d.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="gif" xlink:href="95-6603384f1"/></fig><fig id="fig2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Senescence associated-<italic>&#x003b2;-</italic>galactosidase expression in serially passaged primary and immortalised mammary epithelial cells. Mean SA-<italic>&#x003b2;</italic>-Gal expression is shown &#x000b1;s.d.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="gif" xlink:href="95-6603384f2"/></fig><fig id="fig3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p><italic>p16</italic> and <italic>p21</italic> expression in successively passaged breast cells. Histograms show the relative gene expression for <italic>p16</italic> and <italic>p21</italic> relative to <italic>18&#x02009;s rRNA</italic> expression in primary cell cultures and immortalized breast cell lines. Data is plotted as means&#x000b1;s.em.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="gif" xlink:href="95-6603384f3"/></fig><fig id="fig4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p><italic>SIRT7</italic> expression in normal and breast cancer biopsies. HIstograms show relative sirtuin gene expression in nonmalignant and malignant breast biopsies, relative to an <italic>18&#x02009;s rRNA</italic> control. Data show mean sirtuin expression &#x000b1;s.e.m.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="gif" xlink:href="95-6603384f4"/></fig><fig id="fig5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p><italic>SIRT7</italic> expression in breast biopsies by nodal status. The histogram shows relative sirtuin gene expression in nonmalignant and lymph node-positive and -negative breast cancer biopsies. Data are plotted as mean sirtuin expression &#x000b1;s.e.m.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="gif" xlink:href="95-6603384f5"/></fig><fig id="fig6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p><italic>SIRT3</italic> expression in breast biopsies by nodal status. The histogram shows relative sirtuin gene expression in nonmalignant and lymph node-positive and -negative breast cancer biopsies. Data are plotted as mean sirtuin expression &#x000b1;s.e.m.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="gif" xlink:href="95-6603384f6"/></fig><table-wrap id="tbl1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p content-type="table-title">The primer and probe sequences used in Taqman&#x000ae; PCR.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" border="1" width="85%"><colgroup><col align="left"/><col align="left"/><col align="left"/><col align="left"/></colgroup><thead valign="bottom"><tr><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">&#x000a0;</th><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>Forward primer (5&#x02032;&#x02013;3&#x02032;)</bold></th><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>Reverse primer (5&#x02032;&#x02013;3&#x02032;)</bold></th><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>Probe (5&#x02032; FAM&#x02013;TAMRA 3&#x02032;)</bold></th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50"><italic>SIRT1</italic></td><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">TAGAGCCTCACATGCAAGCTCTA</td><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">GCCAATCATAAGATGTTGCTGAAC</td><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">ACTCCAAGGCCACGGATAGGTCCATATACTT</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50"><italic>SIRT2</italic></td><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">CCTCGCCTGCTCATCAACA</td><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">TCCTCCGAGGCCCATAATC</td><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">TGGCCAGTCGGACCCTTT</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50"><italic>SIRT3</italic></td><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">CATTCGGGCTGACGTGATG</td><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">AACCACATGCAGCAAGAACCT</td><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">TGCACCGGCGTTGTGAAGCC</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" charoff